<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">José Basco y Vargas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1733–<a href="/years/1805">1805</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">José Basco y Vargas was a Spanish naval officer who served as governor-general of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> from 1778 to 1787. He sought to make the colony pay for itself, founding the Economic Society of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quakers">Friends</a> of the Country in 1781 and introducing a government tobacco monopoly in 1782. The monopoly brought large revenues but was deeply resented by Filipino farmers.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jose-basco-y-vargas.jpg" alt="José Basco y Vargas" width="320" height="407" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unidentified painter · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jos%C3%A9_Basco_y_Vargas.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
